course: dictionary
lesson: 9322
title: Sphagnum moss
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Sphagnum is a kind of moss, a small green plant that grows in wet places. The word is hard, but the sentence gives you clues: «Sphagnum, or bog moss, has big empty cells that fill with water.» Bog moss is a clue. A bog is soft, wet ground, so sphagnum is a moss that grows there.
explain: Read on for more clues. The moss can hold up to about 20 times its own dry weight, so it soaks up water very well. It also makes the bog wet and acidic, which means sour, like lemon juice. The sentence shows that sphagnum belongs in wet, sour ground.
explain: To check your guess, swap in the meaning: «a soft wet moss that soaks up water in a bog has big empty cells that fill with water.» Does it still make sense? Yes, so your meaning fits the context.
fact: Sphagnum is a soft moss that grows in wet bogs.
fact: Sphagnum can hold up to about 20 times its own dry weight in water.
fact: Over a very long time, sphagnum slowly turns into peat.
fact: Peat bogs store a lot of carbon, which helps fight climate change. Digging or draining bogs releases that carbon.
fact: In the First World War, sphagnum was used as a wound dressing because it soaks up liquid and slows germs.
exercise: «Sphagnum, or bog moss, has big empty cells that fill with water.» What is sphagnum? | A kind of hard rock found on hills | A kind of sand from the seaside | *A kind of soft moss that grows in wet bogs | A small wild bird
exercise: The sentence says sphagnum can hold up to about 20 times its own dry weight. What does that tell you? | *It soaks up a lot of water | It is dangerous to touch | It only grows in deserts | It is very light and dry
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Sphagnum, or bog moss, has big empty cells that fill with water.» that means the wet, soft ground where moss grows. | water | cells | empty | *bog
test: In the First World War, why was sphagnum used on wounds? | It kept soldiers warm in the snow | *It soaks up liquid and slows germs | It was used to light fires | It made a soft bed for the wounded
test: Why do bogs matter for climate change? | They are always dry and cold | They are full of fish | *They store a lot of carbon | They make the air hotter by burning
exam: «...and it makes the bog wet and acidic.» Using the context, what does acidic most likely mean? | Frozen solid | Warm and dry | *Sour, like lemon juice | Sweet, like sugar
